answer.worked:
Let $\alpha=\frac{-b+\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}$ and $\beta=\frac{-b-\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}$, where $a\neq0$ since the equation is quadratic.
$$\begin{align*}
\alpha+\beta &= \frac{-2b}{2a} = -\frac{b}{a} \\
\alpha\beta &= \frac{(-b)^2-\left(\sqrt{b^2-4ac}\right)^2}{4a^2} \\
&= \frac{b^2-(b^2-4ac)}{4a^2} \\
&= \frac{4ac}{4a^2} = \frac{c}{a}
\end{align*}$$
Since $c\neq0$, $\alpha\beta=\frac{c}{a}\neq0$, so neither root is zero and both reciprocals exist.
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\alpha}+\frac{1}{\beta} &= \frac{\alpha+\beta}{\alpha\beta} \\
&= \frac{-\frac{b}{a}}{\frac{c}{a}} \\
&= -\frac{b}{c}
\end{align*}$$
